⚠️ **IMPORTANT: gh-1350 census cross-check audit line now reports the corrected census instead of the lie it caught**

In `action()`, the reassignment happens **before** the warning is built (`js/postPRReviewComments.js:2548`):

```js
if (censusCrossCheck.overridden) {
    issueCounts = censusCrossCheck.issueCounts;   // blocking already bumped to >= 1 here
    console.warn(buildCensusCrossCheckWarning(issueCounts));
}
```

`buildCensusCrossCheckWarning` prints `issueCounts.blocking`, so the gh-1350 audit line — whose entire purpose is to name the lying census it caught — now emits a self-contradictory message in exactly the corruption scenario it exists for:

```
🚨 Census cross-check (gh-1350): a posted inline comment carries severity BLOCKING
but issueCounts.blocking=1 — overriding the verdict to REQUEST_CHANGES. ...
```

On `main` the warning was built from the original census (`blocking=0`), which is the diagnostic value. The unit test only exercises the pure function with an uncorrected census (`test_postPRReviewComments.js:3753` asserts `blocking=0`), so no test covered the action-level reorder and the regression slipped through.

Correcting the census for the machine recomputation is right — just capture the warning before the reassignment:

```js
if (censusCrossCheck.overridden) {
    var crossCheckWarning = buildCensusCrossCheckWarning(issueCounts);
    issueCounts = censusCrossCheck.issueCounts;
    console.warn(crossCheckWarning);
}
```

and add an action-level assertion on the warning text (it should still say `blocking=0` while the verdict record carries the corrected census).

🪶 **NIT: censusCrossCheckOverride lost its doc comment to the new helper**

The long `censusCrossCheckOverride` JSDoc block (starting "The census cross-check (pure): any posted inline severity BLOCKING with `issueCounts.blocking == 0` overrides an approving verdict…") now sits directly above `correctedCensusOf`, because the new helper — with its own one-liner — was inserted between the block and the function it describes. `censusCrossCheckOverride` itself is left undocumented, and the placement implies the long contract belongs to `correctedCensusOf`.

Reorder so each function keeps its own docs:

```js
/** The census copy the machine recomputation reads: posted evidence bumps blocking to ≥ 1. */
function correctedCensusOf(issueCounts) { … }

/**
 * The census cross-check (pure): any posted inline severity BLOCKING with …
 */
function censusCrossCheckOverride(reviewData, recommendation, issueCounts) { … }
```

